# Managed by dockyard — edit upstream, not here, except inside a keep region you open.
#
# Order matters in a .dockerignore: a later rule wins over an earlier one, so a project's
# own `!path` re-inclusions go in a keep region opened after the rules they override.
#
# Every pattern below is `**/`-prefixed, and that is not decorative. .dockerignore matches
# with Go's filepath.Match, where `*` does not cross a path separator: a bare pattern such
# as `__pycache__` or `tests/` excludes the build context **root only** — measured with a
# real `docker build --output type=local` (see docs/architecture.md). In a monorepo (e.g.
# doc-station's `libs/*/src`, `pipelines/*/src`) a bare pattern lets every nested cache,
# virtualenv and secret ship straight into the image. `**/` is a complete replacement for
# the bare form, not an addition to it — do not "tidy" it back out. There is no exception,
# `.git` included: a git worktree puts a `.git` FILE inside each worktree, so a repo using
# Claude Code worktrees carries one per worktree well below the root. A trailing slash on
# a directory pattern is not a directory-only restriction here the way it is in
# .gitignore — Docker cleans the pattern before matching, so `**/.git/` and `**/.git` are
# the same rule and the worktree's `.git` file is still excluded.
#
# This file's whole body below is
# rendered from exclude.toml's [groups] table by `dockyard sync`, one heading per
# group, in declaration order: edit a group there, not a pattern here.

# build-outputs
**/dist/
**/build/
**/target/

# version-control
**/.git/
**/.hg/
**/.svn/
**/.bzr/

# repo-metadata
**/.gitignore
**/tests/
**/docs/
**/*.md
!**/README.md

# secrets
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# agent-config-in-build-context
**/.claude/
**/.vibe/

# editor-os
**/.DS_Store
**/.vscode/
**/.idea/

# build-recipe
**/Dockerfile
**/docker-compose*.yml
